    "cover_text": "The museum opened a new room about the history of local transportation. As the lights were lowered to reveal the display case next to it—a sprawling network of brick-and-mortar tracks stretching from the old textile mill to the river embankment—thousands of visitors slowly shuffled down the narrow aisle. To anyone passing by without a ticket or a guide book it was easy to mistake for a dusty back alcove of a Victorian garage. But the air grew cool and smelled faintly of ozone and old gasoline just as the doors groaned open on their rusty hinges. Two volunteers stepped forward from the shadows of the archway to guide the sparse crowd they were managing; among the throng of curious onlookers there were a few determined young men with battered hiking boots suited perfectly for the terrain. They didn't just look at the model train replicas or the spinning brass gears displayed neatly on their glass cases; instead they walked with purpose towards a raised drape holding a collection",
